# Agent orchestration and capability tiers

This file's instructions are duplicated, pithily, into `coordinator-core.md` for a harness that cannot load this file directly.
TheForge's coordinator-core ADR records why that file exists and what must survive every edit to it.
A change here updates `.forge/coordinator-core.md` in the same change.
TheForge's `lock` check fails until the core section citing this one is re-read and the lock refreshed.

## Roles

**The owner** decides what the project does and how it is built: product scope, requirement wording, the principles, the lifecycle document's wording, what is parked, and when a release happens.
Agents propose; the owner decides, and the decision is recorded where the process says.

**The coordinator** is the agent session that talks with the owner, keeps the work queue, starts and briefs threads, hands out tickets and reports progress; it arranges work and never builds, designs, tickets or reviews itself.
A session acting as coordinator reads `.forge/coordinator-core.md` and `.agents/skills/build-thread-brief/SKILL.md` in full before its first action and again after a context compaction; the core's "Start-of-session self-check" is the authoritative procedure.
The harness's project-level instructions carry the coordinator-core snippet, which tells the coordinator to load that file and is never a substitute for reading it.

**Threads** are agent sessions that each do one kind of work: a build thread implements tickets, a design thread writes designs and runs architecture passes, the Main Health thread watches `main` and orchestrates the post-merge review, and short-lived threads run triage or interviews.
These are roles, not individuals, and their sessions are rotated regularly, because a long session's accumulated context costs tokens on every turn.
Every rule, lesson and procedure lives in the repo, so a fresh session in any role gets the same instructions and loses nothing.

A harness with only one session can still run this process: the session plays each role in turn, and says so when a step asks for a separate thread or sub-agent it cannot start.

## The coordinator-core snippet is not a source of rules

The coordinator's own rules are authoritative in `.forge/coordinator-core.md`; the coordinator skill holds only look-up procedures, and the project-instructions snippet that tells the coordinator to load the core is not a source of rules (SDLC-113).
The core file is held in the repository and checked against a size limit; it ends with a codeword and carries a mid-file check word, both confirmed only from the coordinator's own whole read so a truncated or unread copy is detected; and a mechanical check fails whenever a source rule it condenses changes without the core being re-reviewed in the same change (`.forge/coordinator-core.md`, "Start-of-session self-check").
A re-paste, or any other update of the project instructions, replaces only the sections the coordinator owns, carrying the owner's scope section and the coordinator-notes zone through verbatim (SDLC-118; `.forge/coordinator-core.md`, "Project instructions").
`queue-sweep` check 7 audits the live project instructions against the repository's snippet.

## Capability tiers

Work is matched to the judgement it needs, not a model name, so the rules work in any harness; `.forge/model-tiers.md` maps each tier to a model.

- **design-tier**: design decisions, security reasoning, module-level contracts, and judging whether a seam across several merged slices is shallow, where a wrong answer is expensive and cannot cheaply be re-checked later.
  It covers high-level and detailed design, architecture passes, design-exit passes, and all three modes of the `security-docs` skill.
- **review-tier**: reviewing a batch of already-merged code for bugs a per-PR review structurally cannot see; more judgement than building a slice, less than originating a design.
  It covers the post-merge review's reviewer sub-agents.
- **build-tier**: executing a scoped, already-designed piece of work: implementing one slice at a time with scoped tests, self-review and merge, plus triage, ticketing, coordination and the Main Health thread.
- **mechanical-tier**: tasks with one obviously correct answer and no design judgement, such as a rename, a sweep for a known pattern, or a lookup.
  It covers mechanical sub-tasks delegated out of a build-tier thread, and every read-only sweep or audit.

The tier belongs to the piece of work, not the thread orchestrating it: a build-tier thread needing design-tier or review-tier work hands it to a sub-agent at that tier rather than doing it itself.
`.forge/feature-pipeline.md`, "Which capability tier runs which stage", maps each stage to its tier.

Design, build and independent review are kept in separate hands where the harness allows.
The builder reviews its own work at merge, starting clean-context sub-agents for part of it; the post-merge review and the architecture pass are never run by the agent whose work they cover.
A builder that cannot start those sub-agents asks the thread that started it to start them on its behalf, and that thread never reviews the diff itself, nor merges a PR whose gate steps are not recorded on it (`.forge/review-gate.md`, "A builder with no sub-agent tool asks its parent for the review").
Those parent-started sub-agents stand in for the builder's own; no review by another thread beyond them is required before merge.

## Tier gate

Every skill or stage that needs design-tier or review-tier opens with this gate, worded once here and reused verbatim at each of those entry points, before any other work:

> Before any other work, check the model actually serving this turn against what `.forge/model-tiers.md` maps this tier to.
> In Claude Code on the web, for example, call `get_session` with no id and read `session_context.model` and `external_metadata.last_served_model`; another harness has its own equivalent of "what model is actually running this turn."
> If it is below the mapped model, do no other work on this ticket or stage: report to the coordinator and the owner that this thread must be restarted on the right model, and stop there.
> A thread cannot switch its own model, which is why the gate stops and reports rather than continuing or switching itself.
> Running on a model above the mapped one is not a problem: note it in one line and proceed.

A thread already confirmed on the right tier in the current unbroken session (such as a design thread that has cleared this gate once) does not re-run it for every stage; it re-runs at the start of a fresh thread or sub-agent.

## Scheduling defaults

- At most {{ max_build_threads }} concurrent build threads, which the owner may raise or lower temporarily.
  The limit counts build threads only; Main Health, design, grill, review-pass, sweep and research threads never consume a slot.
- A build thread blocked only on an external party it cannot resolve itself (the owner's decision, an unresolved `needs-decision`, or an unmerged dependency) releases its slot while it waits, so another thread can use it; this does not count against the limit.
  The exemption is narrow: waiting on its own CI run, a test run, or a review of its own PR is not such a block, and the thread keeps its slot.
  Releasing the slot never means going quiet: the thread keeps answering the owner's and the coordinator's questions and doing whatever discovery or drafting the blocker needs, which is not slotted work and never needs a slot.
  It does no further implementation on the ticket until the blocker clears, then it (or its follow-on) waits for a free slot like any queued thread, never preempting one already running.
  A freed slot is itself the trigger to fill it: in the same check that notices the release, hand it to the next queued ticket, rather than waiting for the thread to come back or the next 30-minute check to notice an idle slot.
- One ticket at a time per thread, handed out by the coordinator; a thread never picks its own next ticket.
- After every completed build, the build thread posts its completion with "Tell me or my follow-on thread what's next on the queue", so the coordinator is woken to hand out the next ticket.
- Bug fixes go ahead of feature work.
- The queue is held in the tracker, not anyone's memory: accepted work is labelled `queued`, and a build thread swaps that for `in-flight` when it starts (`docs/agents/triage-labels.md`, "Queue labels").
- Low-risk mechanical work may run at mechanical-tier in one extra concurrent slot.
- Progress reports give times in the owner's timezone ({{ owner_timezone }}), labelled as such.

## Don't manually trigger CI runs

No agent manually triggers or re-runs a CI workflow unless the owner explicitly says to.
The only standing exceptions are the Main Health thread's scheduled full pass, dispatched only when `main` has moved, and its single re-run of a failure that looks infrastructural (`.forge/ci-and-checks.md`).
